public static int minPerfectSquareSum(int n) {
    List<Integer> perfectSquares = new ArrayList<>();
    for (int i = 1; i * i <= n; i++) {
        perfectSquares.add(i * i);
    }
    int[] dp = new int[n + 1];
    for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
        dp[i] = Integer.MAX_VALUE;
        for (int j = 0; j < perfectSquares.size(); j++) {
            if (perfectSquares.get(j) <= i) {
                dp[i] = Math.min(dp[i], dp[i - perfectSquares.get(j)] + 1);
            }
        }
    }
    return dp[n];
}
```
### Explanation
The method "minPerfectSquareSum" first pre-calculates all the perfect squares up to the input value 'n' and stores them in a list called "perfectSquares". It then initializes a dynamic programming table 'dp' with size 'n + 1' and sets each element to 'Integer.MAX_VALUE'.

For each number from 1 to 'n', it then checks if it can be expressed as the sum of the current number and a perfect square (which is stored in 'dp[i - perfectSquares.get(j)]'). If it can, the method updates 'dp[i]' to be the minimum of the current value and the sum of the number of steps needed to reach the current number and the number of steps needed to reach the perfect square. The method then returns 'dp[n]' which represents the minimum number of perfect square numbers that add up to the input value.

